<section class="section" id="what-is-software-architecture">
    <h3>What is Software Architecture?</h3>
    <p>
        Software architecture is the high-level blueprint of a software system. It defines how the
        system is structured, how components interact, and which technical decisions guide development.
    </p>

    <h4>Why it matters</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Helps teams build scalable and maintainable systems.</li>
        <li>Improves performance, reliability, and security decisions early.</li>
        <li>Creates a shared technical vision across developers and stakeholders.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Core architecture concerns</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Separation of responsibilities between components.</li>
        <li>Communication patterns (sync/asynchronous, APIs, messaging).</li>
        <li>Deployment strategy and scalability approach.</li>
        <li>Resilience, observability, and fault isolation.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="note-box">
        <strong>In short:</strong> Architecture is not only about code structure; it is about making
        system-level decisions that support business goals over time.
    </div>
</section>
